2026-08-16T15:21:49Z    [HAS:Net fees] GATE critic: You projected FY2026 by applying the FY2024→FY2025 reported drop (-12.7%, from £1,113.6m to £972.4m) directly to the FY2025 statutory GBP base. That’s the wrong basis: the reported YoY movement embeds FX and working‑day effects. Hays explicitly separates reported vs like‑for‑like (constant currency, working‑day‑adjusted) net fees; a forward forecast should use the LFL trend (with separate FX assumptions) rather than re‑applying the reported GBP change. Using the reported rate risks double‑counting non‑underlying FX/working‑day effects in FY2026.
2026-08-16T15:22:54Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional basic EPS] GATE critic: You anchored FY2026 off an FY2025 EPS of 1.31p that you explicitly tag as "[adjusted] ... (1)"—i.e., an APM (likely pre‑IFRS 16/underlying) rather than the reported pre‑exceptional basic EPS. The required metric is pre‑exceptional basic EPS in GBp on the reported basis. Using an adjusted/pre‑IFRS 16 figure as the base puts the forecast on the wrong basis. This can be checked directly against Hays’ FY2025 results RNS, which discloses both reported pre‑exceptional EPS and adjusted/underlying variants; 1.31p is not the reported pre‑exceptional basic EPS.
2026-08-16T15:23:18Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional operating profit] GATE critic: You ignored the company’s own stated consensus for the target period. Management says “FY2026 pre-exceptional operating profit is expected to be in line with market consensus expectations of £45.2m.” Using 46.538 as the guidance anchor and inventing an “implied target” of 45.325 to average against produces an upward-biased 45.6614. The checkable anchor for FY2026 pre-exceptional operating profit is £45.2m, so your basis is wrong and your result is above that guidance-aligned figure.
